sci:agnotology a owl:NamedIndividual,
        ho:ScienceStudiesTerm ;
    rdfs:label "agnotology"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Proctor & Schiebinger (eds.), *Agnotology* (Stanford UP, 2008); McGoey, *The Unknowers* (Zed, 2019)"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "agnotology"@en,
        "manufactured doubt"@en,
        "strategic ignorance"@en ;
    skos:definition "The study of ignorance deliberately produced or maintained"@en ;
    skos:example "Avoiding data collection on harms that would create obligations"@en ;
    skos:historyNote "\"Agnotology\" coined 1992; tobacco industry's manufactured doubt as the founding case"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "agnotology"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Institutional and strategic"@en ;
    ho:assertedBy "Cognitive Council"@en ;
    ho:citationVerification "Cognitive review, `suppressors` row"@en ;
    ho:discipline "History of science"@en ;
    ho:explanation "Agnotology is the study of ignorance that is produced or maintained, not simply not-yet-known. Proctor developed it from the tobacco industry's efforts to manufacture doubt about the harms of smoking; McGoey extended it to \"strategic ignorance\" in organizations. As a spoke, it gives the suppressed kind its institutional, evidence-based form. Where Minsky describes the mechanism inside one mind, agnotology documents how institutions arrange not to know: funding counter-research, avoiding studies whose results would create obligations, and designing record-keeping so that certain facts never exist on paper. It is graded closeMatch because it describes the same thing as the kind, at the scale the ontology cares about. For AI harm the pattern is plausible and worth watching: companies that avoid measuring harms that would trigger duties, or systems built so certain data are never logged. Any claim about a specific organization needs its own sourced research. The link to HO-II.e matters here: a value that is Undisclosed, held by the party with most to lose, can be evidence of suppression."@en ;
    ho:keyThinkers "Robert Proctor; Londa Schiebinger; Linsey McGoey"@en ;
    ho:mappingGrade "closeMatch"@en ;
    ho:mappingReason "produced ignorance at institutional scale"@en ;
    ho:mapsToProperty ho:suppressedBlindSpot ;
    ho:meaningAsOf "2008"@en .

sci:anomaly a owl:NamedIndividual,
        ho:ScienceStudiesTerm ;
    rdfs:label "anomaly"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Kuhn, *The Structure of Scientific Revolutions* (1962), ch. VI"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "anomaly"@en,
        "outlier"@en,
        "residual"@en ;
    skos:definition "An observation a lens can't explain and tends to set aside"@en ;
    skos:example "Early complaints about an algorithm that fit no recognized harm category"@en ;
    skos:historyNote "Kuhn argued accumulating anomalies precede scientific revolutions"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "anomaly"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Names what is set aside, not why"@en ;
    ho:assertedBy "Physics Council"@en ;
    ho:citationVerification "Physics review, `anomalies` row"@en ;
    ho:discipline "Philosophy of science"@en ;
    ho:explanation "Kuhn argued that normal science sets aside observations its paradigm can't explain, and that these anomalies accumulate until they force a revolution. Curie's pitchblende, more radioactive than its uranium could explain, is the classic case of an anomaly taken seriously. As a spoke, it maps to the negligent kind with relatedMatch, because setting an anomaly aside isn't always negligent: sometimes it is reasonable, and the anomaly is noise. What it adds is a way to see negligence as it forms. Anomalies are the visible residue of a blind spot, the cases the lens couldn't place. For AI harm, they are often individual complaints that fit no recognized harm category, unusual model behaviour, or patterns among users that no lens has a concept for. These are frequently the first evidence of a new kind of harm. Recording anomalies, rather than letting them drop, is how a lens's negligent blind spots become visible before the damage does. It links this hub to `epistemicStanding` in the Assessments group."@en ;
    ho:keyThinkers "Thomas Kuhn; Marie Curie"@en ;
    ho:mappingGrade "relatedMatch"@en ;
    ho:mappingReason "what a paradigm sets aside, which may be negligent or merely premature"@en ;
    ho:mapsToProperty ho:negligentBlindSpot ;
    ho:meaningAsOf "1962"@en .

sci:cargoCultScience a owl:NamedIndividual,
        ho:ScienceStudiesTerm ;
    rdfs:label "cargo cult science"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Feynman, \"Cargo Cult Science\", *Engineering and Science* 37(7) (1974): 10–13"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "cargo cult science"@en,
        "form without substance"@en,
        "scientism"@en ;
    skos:definition "A practice that imitates the forms of rigorous inquiry without the integrity that makes them work"@en ;
    skos:example "AI benchmarks reported to decimal precision on contaminated test sets"@en ;
    skos:historyNote "Feynman's 1974 Caltech commencement address"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "cargo cult science"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Scientific form and method, not stated purpose"@en ;
    ho:assertedBy "Physics Council"@en ;
    ho:citationVerification "Physics review, `cargoCult` row"@en ;
    ho:discipline "Philosophy of science"@en ;
    ho:explanation "Feynman's 1974 commencement address compared some research to islanders building imitation runways in the hope that planes would land: the form of science without the integrity that makes it work. As a spoke, it adds incongruence between method and substance. A lens can carry every outward sign of rigour (metrics, benchmarks, papers, decimal places) while lacking controls, replication and honest reporting of failure. It is graded relatedMatch because it concerns epistemic practice rather than a stated moral purpose, though the two often travel together when a lens's scientific form is what licenses its authority. For AI the risk is high: benchmarks and evaluations are abundant, while independent replication and reported failures are scarce. The spoke links congruence to the Epistemics group, especially Popper's falsifiers and corroboration. Because \"cargo cult\" is an accusation, it must be recorded as an Assessment with `assessedBy`, never as a plain fact about a lens."@en ;
    ho:keyThinkers "Richard Feynman"@en ;
    ho:mappingGrade "relatedMatch"@en ;
    ho:mappingReason "about epistemic form vs substance, not a stated moral purpose"@en ;
    ho:mapsToProperty ho:congruence ;
    ho:meaningAsOf "1974"@en .

soc:sacrificeZone a owl:NamedIndividual,
        ho:SociologyTerm ;
    rdfs:label "sacrifice zone"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Lerner, *Sacrifice Zones* (MIT Press, 2010)"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "sacrifice zone"@en,
        "tolerated cost"@en ;
    skos:definition "A place or community whose harm is accepted for others' benefit"@en ;
    skos:example "Communities near data centres bearing water and power costs (illustrative)"@en ;
    skos:historyNote "Term from Cold War nuclear sites, applied to environmental justice"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "sacrifice zone"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Geographic and community-bound"@en ;
    ho:assertedBy "Sci-Fi Council"@en ;
    ho:citationVerification "Sci-Fi review, `acceptedHarm` row"@en ;
    ho:discipline "Sociology (environmental justice)"@en ;
    ho:explanation "\"Sacrifice zone\" began as a term for Cold War nuclear sites and was taken up by the environmental justice movement for communities exposed to pollution so that others could benefit. Lerner's *Sacrifice Zones* (2010) documents such places in the United States. As a spoke, it is a narrower case of accepted harm: the tolerated cost falls on a particular place and the people who live there. It adds geography and distribution to the accepted kind. An accepted harm is rarely spread evenly; it is usually concentrated where the people bearing it have the least power to refuse. That connects the hub to the Power group, especially `costBearer`, `differentialBurden` and `whoseVoice`. For AI, the physical infrastructure of computation (data centres, power and water use, mining for hardware) raises the same pattern, though any specific claim about a specific place would need its own research before the book uses it. The example in the table is illustrative only."@en ;
    ho:keyThinkers "Steve Lerner"@en ;
    ho:mappingGrade "narrowMatch"@en ;
    ho:mappingReason "accepted harm located in places and communities"@en ;
    ho:mapsToProperty ho:acceptedHarm ;
    ho:meaningAsOf "2010"@en .

