ho:omission a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "omission"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 11"@en,
        "Vocabulary Crosswalk - Concept Hubs and Discipline Schemes (HO-II.d.2), hub hov:omission"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "hub drafted in HO-II.d.2; replaces blindSpot"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    rdfs:range ho:Assessment ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ho:grp_11_obj ;
    skos:definition "Something bearing on a lens's verdicts that the lens leaves out, whether by design, oversight, suppression or choice."@en ;
    skos:example "A benchmark that measures accuracy and leaves out dignity."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Cross-discipline concept hub, with three branches: out of frame, blind spot (negligent or suppressed), accepted harm"@en ;
    ho:boundaryTests "**Out of frame vs blind spot:** could this lens, used with reasonable care, have seen it? **Negligent vs suppressed:** did the lens suspect it and take deliberate steps not to find out (the *Global-Tech* test)? **Blind spot vs accepted harm:** was it seen and traded off?"@en ;
    ho:designStatus "proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)"@en ;
    ho:displayGroup "11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -> PerspectiveType)"@en ;
    ho:explanation "Every lens leaves things out; the question is how and why. The council reviews found four kinds of exclusion that the 0.01 structure lumped together as `blindSpot`. Taking a blind spot in its ordinary sense, something visible but missed, sorts them into three branches. **Out of frame:** excluded by design and not visible to this lens at all, as a map must leave things out (constitutive). **Blind spot:** visible, but missed, either through lack of reasonable care (negligent) or because the lens suspected it and arranged not to look (suppressed). **Accepted harm:** seen and tolerated as the price of a benefit. Each calls for a different response: out-of-frame omissions need another lens to cover them; negligent blind spots need better instruments or care; suppressed ones need transparency, disclosure and whistleblower protection; accepted harms need moral and political argument. Keeping them apart stops the worse kinds hiding behind the better ones, for example \"out of scope\" used as an alibi for suppression. Which branch applies is a judgement, so it is always an Assessment with `assessedBy`. Links: [[HO-005 Assessed Membership and Systematic Omission|HO-005]], [[HO-004 Criticality, Leverage and the Limits of Scope|HO-004]]."@en ;
    ho:glossLine "What a lens leaves out of its verdicts."@en ;
    ho:hubStatus "candidate"@en ;
    ho:tier "★"@en .

ho:suppressedBlindSpot a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "suppressedBlindSpot"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 11"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "suspected, and deliberately not looked at"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    rdfs:range ho:Assessment ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ho:blindSpot ;
    ho:designStatus "proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)"@en ;
    ho:displayGroup "11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -> PerspectiveType)"@en .

ho:AffectedEntity a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "AffectedEntity"@en ;
    skos:definition "formerly Patient; altLabels \"patient\", \"moral patient\" (Decisions_Log 2026-11-18)"@en ;
    ho:designStatus "stub: range target only in this experiment"@en .

ho:developmentalAltitude a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "developmentalAltitude"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 3.2, 11"@en,
        "Vocabulary Crosswalk - Concept Hubs and Discipline Schemes (HO-II.d.2), hub hov:developmentalAltitude"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "an Assessment; scale + assessedBy required; displayed in group 3, belongs to group 11"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    rdfs:range ho:Assessment ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ho:grp_11_obj,
        ho:grp_3_2_obj ;
    skos:definition "How widely and how reflectively a lens's value structure extends concern, independently of the size of the group it addresses."@en ;
    skos:example "Two national AI strategies with the same social scope, one framed around competitive advantage, the other around global welfare."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Cross-discipline concept hub; one of three scope axes with `socialScope` (size) and `moralCircle` (which beings count)"@en ;
    ho:designStatus "proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)"@en ;
    ho:displayGroup "11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -> PerspectiveType)"@en,
        "3.2 scope axes (kept separate)"@en ;
    ho:explanation "Several traditions, working independently, describe the same thing: values that widen from self, to one's own group, to all people, and sometimes beyond. Confucius made *junzi* a rank of cultivation rather than birth; the Stoics described concern widening outward from oneself; Kohlberg measured stages of moral reasoning, and Gilligan answered with stages of care; Wilber's Integral theory takes Gilligan's four stages as the bands egocentric, ethnocentric, worldcentric and kosmocentric. The author's distinction holds across all of them: a tribe-sized lens can hold world-centric values, so altitude is not size. The attribute is powerful because a lens organized around self-interest really does differ from one organized around universal concern, and the difference shapes which harms it counts. It is dangerous because every scale is built from inside a tradition and tends to put that tradition near the top. Gilligan's critique of Kohlberg is the documented case. So the ontology never stores altitude as a bare value: each value belongs to a named scale, is an Assessment with `assessedBy`, and disagreements between scales are recorded as Divergences. For the book, it matters when AI developers claim their systems align to \"universal\" or \"higher\" values: the question is always whose scale."@en ;
    ho:glossLine "How widely and reflectively a lens's values extend concern."@en ;
    ho:hubStatus "candidate"@en .

ho:grp_12b_data a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "12b PROV"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "DISPLAY GROUP (PerspectiveStructure 0.02): an abstract grouping property so the property tree mirrors the diagram. Do not use it in data. Groups are display facets, not OWL classes. Data-property side of the group (OWL keeps the two trees apart)."@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ho:grp_12_data ;
    ho:displayGroup "12b PROV"@en .

ho:grp_4_data a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "4 EXTENT"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "DISPLAY GROUP (PerspectiveStructure 0.02): an abstract grouping property so the property tree mirrors the diagram. Do not use it in data. Groups are display facets, not OWL classes. Data-property side of the group (OWL keeps the two trees apart)."@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    ho:displayGroup "4 EXTENT"@en .

ho:grp_7_data a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "7 STANCE (holder's side)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "DISPLAY GROUP (PerspectiveStructure 0.02): an abstract grouping property so the property tree mirrors the diagram. Do not use it in data. Groups are display facets, not OWL classes. Data-property side of the group (OWL keeps the two trees apart)."@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    ho:displayGroup "7 STANCE (holder's side)"@en .

ho:congruence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "congruence"@en ;
    dcterms:source "PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 11"@en,
        "Vocabulary Crosswalk - Concept Hubs and Discipline Schemes (HO-II.d.2), hub hov:congruence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "stated vs operative; hub drafted in HO-II.d.2"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ho:PerspectiveType ;
    rdfs:range ho:Assessment ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ho:grp_11_obj ;
    skos:definition "The degree to which what a lens says it is and does (its name, stated purpose, declared rules) matches what it actually does in practice."@en ;
    skos:example "A \"trust & safety\" function whose real remit is reputational protection."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Cross-discipline concept hub"@en ;
    ho:designStatus "proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)"@en ;
    ho:displayGroup "11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -> PerspectiveType)"@en ;
    ho:explanation "Five disciplines, working from very different starting points, name the same gap between a lens's declared identity and its actual operation. Law calls it congruence between declared rule and official action; Confucian philosophy, the rectification of names; Kantian ethics, the difference between a professed end and the maxim actually acted on; political theory, the language rules that hide what is done; philosophy of science, cargo cult science, the form of rigour without its substance. The hub gives the idea a plain name so that no discipline's term is privileged, and each spoke keeps its own vocabulary and says where it differs. For the book this is central: many AI harms are carried by names (\"safety\", \"alignment\", \"personalization\") that a practice can claim without earning. Because judging incongruence is an evaluation, the ontology records it as an Assessment with `assessedBy`, never as a plain fact about the lens. The five-way convergence is the strongest in the council reviews, but all five were voiced by one model, so it counts as a candidate, not as independent confirmation."@en ;
    ho:glossLine "The fit between what a lens says it does and what it actually does."@en ;
    ho:hubStatus "candidate"@en ;
    ho:tier "★★"@en .

ho:epistemicStanding a owl:ObjectProperty ;
